    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Slip distributions on normal faults are often asymmetric and display multiple slip maxima. Numerically computed slip distributions from elastic models indicate that those irregular slip variations can be due to mechanical interactions between faults with complex geometry which cause local perturbations of the stress field. A three dimensional boundary element model based on the displacement discontinuity method has been used to analyze the mechanical interaction of faults that form Y- or T-shaped intersections. The slip distribution is asymmetric with a steep slip gradient near the line of intersection. Multiple slip maxima occur depending on the angle between the faults, but generally not at the intersection, nor at the fault centers. Furthermore, direction of slip on the fault plane differs from the expected down-dip direction when close to an intersection line. All these observations based on theoretical results are compared with outcrop data. The very good similarity between field observations and theoretical results not only provides insight into the mechanics of linked faults, but can help to develop methods to refine seismic interpretation and to predict fault linkage, both of which are important to estimate reservoir compartmentalization.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
